nodecode-jwt is a rewrite of olric3015's olric3015/hermes-plugins:

  https://github.com/olric3015/hermes-plugins
  commit 9c842d6da34cce6bce21a43af097629217828634
  MIT License, Copyright (c) 2026 olric3015 (LICENSE)

What was rewritten: the plugin's decoder (tokens.py: the reading of a compact
JWS or JWE, base64url with the padding put back, the header and the claims
read as JSON with a name that appears twice noted, the description of a
token's fields and what their names mean, the algorithm spelled out, the
NumericDates as UTC dates and against the clock, the expired / valid /
not-yet-valid status, and the notes) is rewritten in Common Lisp as decode.lisp,
and its one seam - a @jwt: context reference Hermes expands as a message goes
out - becomes the jwt_decode tool the model calls, /jwt for the operator, and
(help :jwt). The behaviour kept is the original's: 2, 3 and 5 dotted parts;
a header that is not base64url, not JSON or not an object refused with the
part named; a payload that is not a JSON object shown as text with its byte
count; a JWE's header shown and its claims left alone; the status lines with
the same wording (EXPIRED ... ago, valid for ... more, NOT VALID YET, the
lifetime of exp - iat, a NumericDate that looks like milliseconds); and the
same notes (only 2 parts, no alg, alg none, an empty signature, a key set URL
the token points at itself, crit, a claim name twice). Nothing is verified: no
signature, no key, no network, exactly as in the original. The bounds are
the original's: 16,384 characters in, 60 claims listed, 200 characters of one
value.

What was left out, and why: the TUI and desktop suggestion preview (tokens.py's
preview, "@jwt:..." as it is typed) is a seam of that harness's composer and
has no counterpart in Nodecode's - the model calls the tool or the operator
types /jwt. The original pinned the clock-difference that is not worth a
warning at 60 seconds; here that is a setting. Its error strings are reworded
in Lisp, saying the same things.

What the rewrite adds: the jwt section (the four bounds, which the original
hard-coded), /jwt, (help :jwt), a Bearer prefix and one pair of surrounding
quotes stripped from what is handed over, a tool description that teaches the
model when a token is worth decoding, and its own JSON reader, which keeps a
name's order - so a claim written twice is reported rather than silently
overwritten.
